# The preserved gateway bytecode is the ONLY surviving copy of a lost source file
# (see taskmgmt/recovered/README.md). Git's auto-detection gets this right today,
# but this checkout is on a Windows drive and a core.autocrlf setting on some other
# machine would rewrite line endings inside the file and silently corrupt it - and a
# corrupt .pyc cannot be recompiled, because there is no source. Pin it explicitly.
taskmgmt/recovered/*.bin binary
*.pyc                    binary

# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, which gives them CRLF - and bash cannot
# source a CRLF file. Sourcing testlib.sh then fails with a carriage-return
# "command not found" on every line, and every helper it defines goes missing.
#
# That is why the suites are invoked as `bash <(tr -d ...)`: it strips carriage
# returns from the script being RUN. It does NOT strip them from a sibling that
# script SOURCES. test_frontend.sh, check_test_failability.sh and demo_protocol.sh
# all source testlib.sh directly, so on a fresh Windows clone they break before
# their first assertion. Observed 2026-09-25.
#
# Same reasoning as the pinned .pyc above: a line-ending rewrite that looks
# cosmetic silently breaks a file that is executed rather than read.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The task store MUST check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# The task-management plugin writes these documents with newline endings and
# parses them by splitting on the frontmatter fence. Under core.autocrlf they
# check out with CRLF, the fence no longer matches, and every document parses to
# an EMPTY object - so `tm board` renders "undefined undefined" and the board
# looks lost. The .md files are perfectly intact; only the reader breaks.
#
# So a plain `git clone`, a branch switch, or a `git checkout -- .` on a Windows
# machine silently blanks the board. Observed 2026-09-25: EP-002 was CRLF and
# parsed to {}, while a task the plugin had just written was LF and parsed fine.
# Recovery is `tm reindex` once the endings are right.
.bytedesk/task-management/** text eol=lf

# Vendored third-party code MUST check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# field/vendor/MANIFEST.sha256 records a hash per vendored file, and
# dashboard/check_vendor.sh verifies them - that is how "these are the bytes
# that were reviewed" stays checkable offline, on the plant-side boxes where
# nothing can re-download from PyPI.
#
# Under core.autocrlf those .py files check out with CRLF, every hash changes,
# and the vendor gate fails on a fresh Windows clone while nothing is actually
# wrong. Same failure family as the two pins above: a line-ending rewrite that
# looks cosmetic breaks a file that is verified rather than merely read.
#
# It would also silently unpin the dependency, which is the part that matters.
dashboard/vendor/** text eol=lf
field/vendor/**     text eol=lf
